            "content": [
              {
                "type": "output_text",
                "text": "The latest rover-specific news is that NASA’s **Perseverance** has been making notable progress on both science and driving milestones:\n\n- **Ancient impact record found at Jezero’s rim:** NASA reported on July 15, 2026, that Perseverance found evidence that a **245-foot-thick / 75-meter-thick stack of ancient rock** on Jezero Crater’s rim—called the **“Broom Point member”**—was likely built up by **repeated asteroid impacts**. NASA says the layered bedrock may be **more than 3.9 billion years old**【6:1†source】.\n\n- **New Broom Point imagery:** NASA also released a sweeping Mastcam-Z view from **Broom Point**, looking back toward the outside lip of Jezero Crater’s roughly **490-foot / 150-meter-tall rim**【6:1†source】. These images help scientists interpret the rover’s current terrain and plan observations.\n\n- **Perseverance completed a “marathon” on Mars:** The rover recently passed **26.2 miles / 42.195 km** of total driving on Mars, reaching the milestone on **sol 1,890** of its mission. It did so in about **five years and four months**, much faster than Opportunity, which took over 11 years to travel the same distance【6:0†source】.\n\n- **Orbital image captured the milestone:** NASA’s Mars Reconnaissance Orbiter/HiRISE imaged Perseverance as a tiny speck on June 13, 2026, shortly before it reached the marathon-distance mark; the image also shows the rover’s tracks across the Martian surface【6:2†source】.\n\nIn short: Perseverance is now exploring farther west beyond Jezero Crater, returning new views from Broom Point, and uncovering evidence that parts of Jezero’s rim record very ancient impact-driven geology.",
